{"text":"History shuffling is useful, but its interpretation needs controls. [Does It Look Sequential? (RecSys 2024)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.12008) compares original and shuffled histories across 15 datasets, finding weak sequential structure in several popular benchmarks; accompanying experiments include recommendation-list overlap and bootstrap significance checks. [Disentangling User and Item Sequence Patterns (RecSys 2025)](https://doi.org/10.1145/3705328.3748042) identifies a major confound: arbitrary shuffling mixes user-behavior dependencies with item availability. Its constrained shuffle prevents interactions preceding an item’s first observed appearance. [Measuring Recency Bias (2024)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.09722) separately measures excessive emphasis on recent interactions and reports improvements from mitigating it.

Architecture and evaluation offer complementary evidence. [CETRec (2025)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.03047), studying LLM recommenders, distinguishes absolute recency from relative order and connects temporal sensitivity to positional representations and training objectives; transfer to SASRec requires direct ablation. [Beyond Top-1 (IJCAI 2026)](https://ijcai.org/proceedings/2026/926) finds that evaluating perturbations across recommendation lists can improve consistency over top-item-only evaluation, with sensitivity to model quality and metric choice.

Suggested synthesis: pair recent-event removal with equally sized older/random removals matched on training popularity and title/description similarity; compare isolated-event removal against repeated-interest removal. Shuffle older history while retaining the latest event, then compare with full and availability-constrained shuffles. Report paired rank changes, top-10 overlap, popularity shifts, and user-bootstrap intervals separately by dataset. Control padding and position reassignment, and ablate positional embeddings and training signals before attributing effects to attention. These are proposed model-sensitivity probes: perturbed histories provide no observed counterfactual preference labels or evidence of unobserved conformity traits."}
